  "why": "The statement names no conjecture, so I read it as covering the several conjectures attributed to Hartshorne. Any result on one of them, or on a restriction of one, is therefore at most a case. After the snapshot, Park (a new degree-bounded range of the complete-intersection conjecture B) and Kummini–Subramanian (a restricted codimension-2 subcanonical class) are cases. Neither is reviewed, and the journal link for Park is unverified. Benoist–Voisin is a conditional incompatibility and Tipler a reduction; both are none. Watanabe works below B's range, Ghosh proves Kovács's conjecture, and Dosea et al. treat a generalised cofiniteness question; all three are variants. The OpenAI manuscript proves the Campana–Peternell conjecture in dimension six. That is a different conjecture, the manuscript never mentions Hartshorne, and the only bearing it could have, on the ample-tangent-bundle statement, is through a result Mori settled in 1979. I scored it variant. By its README the model generated the work, so its AI role is generated. Before the snapshot, Mori settles candidate D only, so it is a case, not the whole, of a plural statement. De Jong's blog post is a counterexample to E as originally stated: a case, refuted. That post counts as digested because a named expert worked through it publicly.",
  "doubts": "The referent of the bullet cannot be determined: the frozen wikitext has no link or reference, and the paper cited in the live footnote (Barlet–Peternell–Schneider) was not read. If the bullet means only candidate D, Mori 1979 would make the problem resolved before the snapshot. Park's part (i) is a reproof of Ballico–Chiantini; only its degree-bounded theorem is new. The journal acceptances recorded for Ellia–Franco and for Park come from search snippets, so I scored both unreviewed.",
